SersicPhotometry
2015-12-17
Lee Kelvin <l.s.kelvin@ljmu.ac.uk>

The SersicPhotometry DMU produces a single-Sersic (1-component only)
fit to the 2D surface brightness distribution of every object in the
GAMA II equatorial survey regions (i.e. every object in TilingCatv41).
The fitting is performed across all passbands in the SDSS (ugriz),
UKIDSS-LAS (YJHK) and VISTA VIKING (ZYJHK) surveys. This DMU contains
three catalogues, one for each survey: SersicCatSDSS, SersicCatUKIDSS
and SersicCatVIKING.

Galaxy single-Sersic modelling is achieved using SIGMA v1.0-2
(Structural Investigation of Galaxies via Model Analysis). SIGMA is a
wrapper around several contemporary astronomy tools such as Source
Extractor (<a href="https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1996A%26AS..117..393B">Bertin & Arnouts 1996</a>), PSF Extractor (Bertin and Delorme,
priv. comm.) and GALFIT 3 (<a href="https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2010AJ....139.2097P">Peng et al. 2010</a>), as well as analysis and
processing code written in the open-source <a href="http://www.r-project.org/">R programming language</a> (R
Development Core Team, 2010). Please cite <a href="https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2012MNRAS.421.1007K">Kelvin et al. (2012)</a> when
using data products from this DMU.

GALFIT is the workhorse fitting algorithm used within SIGMA. To
produce a model of a galaxy, GALFIT must be provided with suitable
starting parameters (such as size, magnitude, ellipticity, etc.), an
appropriate PSF and cutouts of both the science data and any
associated weight maps. The cutouts are created from the GAMA v03
mosaics, with physical initial conditions and the PSF provided by
Source Extractor and PSF Extractor, respectively. These programs are
called and setup automatically within the SIGMA script, their outputs
reprocessed into a form GALFIT is able to read, and fed into GALFIT.
GALFIT uses a simple downhill minimisation in order to minimise the
global chi^2 of the model. Its outputs are analysed by SIGMA for any
obvious errors, and, if found, an attempt at fixing them will
automatically be made.

The catalogues within this DMU do not contain many of the calculated
Source Extractor and PSF Extractor values used as an input into
GALFIT. Instead, only the main Source Extractor and PSF Extractor
values are included, alongside the full GALFIT outputs. In addition,
several utility parameters (such as truncated magnitude and central
surface brightness, etc.) are estimated based on the GALFIT outputs,
and provided here, for convenience.

Below are more detailed notes on several of the columns provided by
the catalogues of the SersicPhotometry DMU. The unique identifier for
the SersicPhotometry DMU is the CATAID (column 1) which refers to the
GAMA ID of an object. Null entries in any of the catalogues are
labelled -9999. For further details on the operation of SIGMA, please
refer to Kelvin et al. (2012).

* The Mosaicing DMU has not yet undergone Quality Control, however,
  its outputs will not change in the foreseeable future.

Dependencies:
    InputCat    TilingCatv41
    Mosaicing   v03 (non-QC'ed product)

################################################################################

SURVEY_OLDCLASS (from TilingCatv41)

soc = 0:  not a target (except for checks on vis_class=3).
soc = 1:  filler targets - 19.8-19.85, multi-lambda (G12).
soc = 2:  H-ATLAS targets.
soc >= 3: *** MS *** union of GAMA II main survey and GAMA I main survey.
soc >= 4: GAMA I main survey.
soc >= 5: GAMA I main survey r or z-selected. 
soc >= 6: GAMA I main survey r-selected only. 
soc = 7:  r < 19.0 and sg_sep > 0.25 (Year 1 high priority targets).

################################################################################

SURVEY_CLASS (from TilingCatv41)

sc = 0:  not a target (except for checks on vis_class=3).
sc = 1:  filler targets - 19.8-19.85, multi-lambda (G12).
sc = 2:  H-ATLAS targets.
sc >= 3: *** MS *** union of GAMA II main survey and GAMA I main survey.
sc >= 4: r < 19.8, GAMA II main survey.
sc >= 5: r < 19.8 and satisfies r-band star-galaxy separation.
sc = 6:  r < 19.4 and satisfies r-band star-galaxy separation.

See 'Input catalogue and star-galaxy separation' (IC) paper for
details on selection.

Further note: Objects with target_flags=0 (also survey_class=0) have 
2 <= vis_class <= 4 or they are excluded only by Eq. 12 of the IC
paper (Sec. 5.2). These remain in the tiling catalogue because they
are rejected by a more subjective criteria than the other selections.

################################################################################

Position Angles / GALPA

The position angle on the image recovered from GALFIT. This is defined
from the positive x-axis of the image (approximately aligned to west),
increasing from zero anti-clockwise.

x-positive     0 degrees
y-positive    90 degrees
x-negative   180 degrees (equivalent to x-positive, redundant)
y-negative   -90 degrees (equivalent to y-positive, redundant)

This DMU does *not* include equivalent WCS angles on the sky. However,
an approximate on-sky angle can be recovered as:

GALPA_J2000_X = GALPA_X + (THETA_J2000 - THETA_IMAGE)

where THETA_J2000 and THETA_IMAGE are the on-sky and on-image position
angles, respectively, listed in the table <a href="/dr4/schema/table.php?name=ApMatchedCat">ApMatchedCat</a> in the
ApMatchedPhotom DMU.

Despite this being only an approximation, we recommend the use of
GALPA_J2000_X over THETA_J2000.

################################################################################

GALPLAN

From time to time, giving GALFIT too much freedom whilst fitting
(e.g., attempting to fit >5 secondary Sérsic sources at the same time
as the primary galaxy) causes the GALFIT code to crash. In cases like
this, we re-run GALFIT with a different set of initial conditions; in
essence, an automatic hotfix.  These hotfixes, or GALFIT plans
(GALPLAN) are tried in a sequential order until one of them
successfully produces a stable GALFIT output file. Below we list the
GALPLAN flag identifiers, and their meaning.

0  - All hotfixes below have been attempted, without success
1  - standard setup, SExtractor used as input
2  - shrink fitting region (only if a very bright nearby neighbour is detected)
3a - jiggle initial guess fitting parameters down (smaller/fainter)
3b - jiggle initial guess fitting parameters up (larger/brighter)
4  - mask all secondary sources
5  - remove all fitting constraints

Note that GALPLAN 3a and 3b appear simply as 3 in the output
catalogues.

If the first fit fails, and a sufficiently bright nearby source is not
automatically detected, SIGMA skips to 3b and alters the SExtractor
inputs by mag+1 and radius*0.9 ('jiggle' down).

If GALPLAN 3a is unsuccessful, SIGMA skips to 3b and alters the
SExtractor inputs by mag-1 and radius*1.1 ('jiggle' up).

These hotfixes can significantly help as, often, GALFIT only needs a
little assistance getting out of a local minimum that it had
previously been trapped in. However, we caution that just because
GALFIT has created an output file without crashing does not mean that
the model is a good description of the galaxy.

################################################################################

Goodness of Fit

The SersicPhotometry catalogues provide two main estimators of
goodness of fit: GALCHI2 and PRICHI2, two measures of the reduced
chi^2 statistic, in addition to their associated parameters (number of
free parameters, number of degrees of freedom, and full unreduced
chi^2 statistic).

The GALCHI2 values represent the GALFIT goodness of fit statistic.
This statistic is derived from all pixels in the entire input fitting
region, including secondary objects.

The PRICHI2 values represent derived goodness of fit statistics based
on only those pixels which belong to the primary galaxy Source
Extractor segmentation map. As such, PRICHI2 is intended to be a more
reliable goodness of fit statistic to use for model quality
estimation, and is recommended for science use.

################################################################################

Magnitudes

Magnitudes presented in this catalogue have not been corrected for the
effect of foreground Milky Way dust extinction. Should these
corrections be required, they can be obtained from the table
<a href="/dr4/schema/table.php?name=GalacticExtinction">GalacticExtinction</a> in the EqInputCat DMU.

All photometry is on the AB system.

################################################################################

FAQs

Q: How would you recommend to exclude bad fits from the
SersicPhotometry catalogues, in each band, and why?

A: First up, you can use the goodness of fit estimators to gain an
insight into the quality of the fit. Each galaxy has two goodness of
fit estimators: GALCHI2 and PRICHI2. GALCHI2 is the reduced
chi-squared as output by GALFIT. PRICHI2 is the recalculated reduced
chi-squared as calculated by SIGMA, using only those pixels which
belong to the primary galaxy (as determined by the Source Extractor
segmentation map).

The catalogues also give the number of degrees of freedom
(GALNDOF/PRINDOF = number of pixels - number of fitted parameters =
pixels - 7) and the total (non-reduced) chi-squared values
(GALCHI2FULL/PRICHI2FULL), which should allow you to calculate a
p-value for each galaxy, should you wish to do so.

Choosing the thresholds for what may be considered a 'good' reduced
chi-squared is best left to the user. We recommend using the range 
0.5 < chi-squared-reduced < 1.5 (or 2), but other options are
available (e.g., exclude the outlying 10%, say). In general, we find
the primary goodness of fit estimators (PRI*) tend to be more
sensitive to the fit of the primary galaxy. For science therefore, we
generally recommend their use over the GALFIT values, which instead
tell you more about the quality of the model fit to the image as a
whole (i.e., including secondary and tertiary galaxies as well).

In addition, GALFIT also outputs statistical (Poisson based) errors
for each of the main measurables. These may be used to gauge the
robustness of the output parameters.

There's also the quality of the PSF to consider. If the PSF is badly
modelled, it increases the chance that the galaxy will be badly
modelled also. To correct for this, you may consider removing galaxies
with a bad PSF model fit using either the goodness of fit estimator
(PSFCHI2) or the number of stars used to make the PSF (PSFNUM). In
general, we advise caution when using PSF models created using less
than 10 prototype stars.

Next up, there is cross comparison with other photometry methods. If
the Sérsic model is recovering significantly different flux from an
aperture based model, something has gone wrong. The possibilities are
either a) the Sérsic model is completely erroneous, and has converged
on something meaningless, or, b), the aperture is contaminated
somehow, usually due to an overwhelmingly large aperture radius (e.g.,
crowded field, nearby star), or the galaxy is too faint to generate an
accurate aperture size. In any event, when these two numbers disagree
by more than 50%, it speaks to the quality of the input data, which
then impacts the quality of the output model, either for Sersic
photometry or other complementary photometry methods. We advise
against using these values, if possible.

Finally, the 'best' way to check a model fit is to have a look at the
1D light profile for your object of interest, and overplot the
recovered Sersic profile. Looking at catalogue outputs to try and
gauge the goodness of fit for any given individual object is never a
substitute for actually looking at the data.

In summary: Utilising the (primary) chi^2 of both the galaxy and the
PSF, cross-comparison with different photometric methods, and
inspecting the 1D surface brightness profiles for small sample checks
should allow for bad fits to easily be identified and removed.


Q: Some galaxies are consistently +1 or -1 mag brighter than their
Petrosian or Kron magnitudes; why is this?

A: One of the problems with the previous SersicPhotometryv07 DMU was
the relatively small size of some of the fitting regions. On occasion,
the flux in the wings of galaxies was erroneously missed, impacting
the ability to successfully model this flux. To that end, for the
SersicPhotometryv09 DMU we have increased the size of the initial
fitting region. This successfully includes more flux from the primary
galaxy, however, it also includes more flux from nearby neighbouring
sources such as galaxies or foreground stars. This can, on occasion,
cause fitting problems for the GALFIT software, as we are increasing
the complexity of the model fit for each additional component we wish
to model.

If the standard model fit fails (GALPLAN 1), and a bright nearby star
is detected, SIGMA will attempt GALPLAN 3a and 3b (modifying the
initial SExtractor inputs by mag +1/-1 and radius *0.9/*1.1). For
particularly extreme cases, the GALFIT software determines that it
hasn't been given enough information to calculate the local gradient
in probability space, or, more likely, the starting parameters are now
so incorrect that it has no chance of calculating an initial gradient
with which to start the minimisation procedure. In this scenario, the
code will finish, and the output catalogue for this object will show a
galaxy with a magnitude of the Source Extractor input mag+1 or mag-1.

Testing shows that this problem is minimal (< ~1%) and, in any event,
only has an impact for the Sersic photometry of objects in close
proximity to very bright nearby sources.

